 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection algorithm in MATLAB, you need to add an extra step to your existing code. Here's how you can modify your code to use a Gaussian filter of size 3x3 pixels before applying the Sobel operator:
```MATLAB
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Define Gaussian filter
gaussian_filter = fspecial('gaussian', [3 3], 0.5);
% Apply Gaussian filter to grayscale image
filtered_image = imfilter(gray_image, gaussian_filter, 'same');
% Define Sobel operator in x direction
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
% Define Sobel operator in y direction
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction to filtered image
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction to filtered image
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```
This modified code first applies a Gaussian filter using the `imfilter()` function with a standard deviation of 0.5 for a 3x3 filter. Then, it applies the Sobel operator on the filtered image instead of the original grayscale image. This helps to reduce the noise in the image and improve the accuracy of the edge detection results.

 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection in MATLAB, you can use the `imgaussfilt` function to apply the Gaussian filter and then apply the Sobel operator on the filtered image. Here's the modified code:
