 To write a Bash script that downloads JPG files from the given CSV file with the specified name convention, concurrently with a rate limit of 10 files per second, follow these steps:

1. First, install `csvkit` to parse the CSV file if not already installed:
   ```
   sudo apt-get install csvkit -y
   ```
2. Create a new Bash script file called `download_images.sh` and paste the following content:

```bash
#!/bin/bash

# Set variables
CSV_FILE="input.csv"
CONCURRENT_DOWNLOADS=10
DOWNLOAD_DIR="downloads"
UUID=$(cat /dev/urandom | tr -dc 'a-z0-9' | fold -w 32 | head -n 1)

# Create download directory if it doesn't exist
mkdir -p ${DOWNLOAD_DIR}

# Parse CSV file using csvformat
csvformat --header-file <(printf "id,after_rendition\n") "${CSV_FILE}" |
awk -F, '
BEGIN {
    FD["after_rendition"] = "stdin";
    next;
}
{ print >("download.tmp"), $2 }
END {
    close("download.tmp");
}
' |
xargs -I{} sh -c '
if [ ! -f "${DOWNLOAD_DIR}/{}" ]; then
    wget --quiet --rate-limit=10K --background-threads=${CONCURRENT_DOWNLOADS} \
        {} -P ${DOWNLOAD_DIR}/ -O "{}.jpg";
fi
'

# Rename the downloaded files with UUID as prefix
for i in $(find ${DOWNLOAD_DIR} -type f -name "*jpg"); do
    mv "$i" "${DOWNLOAD_DIR}/${UUID}-$(basename "$i")";
done

echo "Download completed. Check ${DOWNLOAD_DIR} for the downloaded images."
```

