 To create an efficient NoSQL document schema for a hotel room reservation system running on MongoDB with the given requirements, we'll structure our data as follows:

```javascript
// Define the Hotel model
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const Schema = mongoose.Schema;

const hotelSchema = new Schema({
  name: { type: String, required: true }, // Name of the hotel
  location: { type: String, required: true }, // Address and coordinates of the hotel
  numberOfRooms: { type: Number, default: 0 }, // Total number of rooms in the hotel
  createdAt: { type: Date, default: Date.now }, // Creation date of the hotel record

  rooms: [{ // Array of room objects
    type: Schema.Types.ObjectId,
    ref: 'Room',
  }], // Reference to Room model
});

module.exports = mongoose.model('Hotel', hotelSchema);

// Define the Room model
const roomSchema = new Schema({
  number: { type: Number, required: true, unique: true }, // Room number
  capacity: { type: Number, required: true }, // Maximum number of guests this room can accommodate
  pricePerNight: { type: Number, required: true }, // Base price per night
  status: { type: String, enum: ['available', 'reserved', 'maintenance'], default: 'available' }, // Status of the room
  reservations: [{ // Array of reservation objects
    guestName: String, // Name of the guest
    checkInDate: Date, // Check-in date
    checkOutDate: Date, // Check-out date
    createdAt: { type: Date, default: Date.now }, // Creation date of the reservation record
  }],
});

roomSchema.index({ number: 1 }); // Index on room number for faster lookups

module.exports = mongoose.model('Room', roomSchema);
```

This schema has two main models - `Hotel` and `Room`. Each `Hotel` document stores an array of `Room` documents as a reference. This design allows us to efficiently manage multiple hotels with their respective rooms using MongoDB's document-oriented nature.

