 To use `react-native-fbsdk-next` in your JavaScript code and pass your Facebook credentials, follow these steps:

First, install the package by running the command:

```bash
npm install react-native-fbsdk-next
``` or

```bash
yarn add react-native-fbsdk-next
```

Next, link the library to your project (for React Native 0.59 and below):

```bash
# For React Native 0.59 and below
react-native link react-native-fbsdk-next
```

Now, you can import and initialize the Facebook SDK with your app ID:

```javascript
import { LoginManager, AccessToken } from 'react-native-fbsdk-next';

// Replace YOUR_APP_ID with your actual App ID
LoginManager.logInWithPermissions(["public_profile", "email"]).then(function(result) {
  if (result.isCancelled) {
    console.log("Login cancelled");
  } else {
    accessToken = result.accessToken.getCurrentAccessToken();
    console.log(
      "Login success with permissions: " +
        result.grantedPermissions.toString()
    );
  }
});
```

You do not need to expose your App Secret in your client-side code as it's only required for server-to-server communication. Instead, you will be using the `accessToken` obtained during the login process to make API requests on behalf of the user.

Keep in mind that this is just a basic example. Depending on your use case, you might want to handle errors, check if the user is already logged in, or manage multiple users in your application. The official documentation for `react-native-fbsdk-next` provides more detailed information: https://github.com/mreaks/react-native-fbsdk-next

