 To create a JavaFX application for the given Advanced OOP Assignment II, follow these steps:

1. Import the provided Eclipse project into your favorite IDE (I assume you're using IntelliJ IDEA in this explanation).
2. Create a new JavaFX project in your IDE with your name in the project name.
3. Copy all the packages with their files (excluding the test class), the `data` folder, and the required libraries to the new project.
4. In the new project, create a class named `Main.java` with the following code:

```java
import javafx.application.Application;
import javafx.scene.Scene;
import javafx.stage.Stage;
import javafx.scene.Parent;
import edu.wisc.starcollections.*; // Import StarCollection
import edu.wisc.speciescollections.*; // Import SpeciesCollection

public class Main extends Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        launch(args);
    }

    @Override
    public void start(Stage primaryStage) throws Exception {
        StarCollection starCollection = StarCollection.getInstance();
        starCollection.loadStarsFromFile();

        SpeciesCollection speciesCollection = SpeciesCollection.getInstance();
        speciesCollection.loadSpeciesFromFile();

        Parent root = FXMLLoader.load(getClass().getResource("main.fxml"));

        Scene scene = new Scene(root, 800, 600);
        primaryStage.setTitle("Galaxy Explorer");
        primaryStage.setScene(scene);
        primaryStage.show();
    }
}
```

Replace `main.fxml` with the path to your main FXML file.

5. Create an FXML file named `main.fxml` with the desired menu structure and layout for the application. Use the Scene Builder or any XML editor to build the layout.
6. Implement the functionality for each menu option as described in the assignment instructions (3a-3g).
7. Don't forget to update the FXML file accordingly.

Now you should have a functional JavaFX application with a graphical user interface for exploring the galaxy data. Remember to test each feature as you go

